whose own served sample decides shipping. The finding the design rests on is narrow and has now been seen twice. When
a call has been refused twice with advice that names the refused call, luna at effort none stops calling tools and
writes a short text (chat 31 of 40, Responses 40 of 40); at effort low it investigates (40 of 40), for about ten
reasoning tokens and a few tenths of a second. In a served loop a text where a call was needed is a stall. That is
one decision point of five. The other point where the arms differ, order, shows no gain over today's product.
Constraints the design inherits:
  1  A Responses route must not run this tier at effort none: C is the worst arm in all four cell-batches.
  2  "Route" here is the endpoint AND my rendering: the system text rides in the instructions field and the tools
     are flattened. Which of the two moves order is measured first, in a stamped block of its own.
  3  This bench scores one response. Carrying reasoning items across a tool loop, prompt-cache behaviour on the
     Responses route (ADR-0193 was measured on chat) and streamed cost accounting (ADR-0195) are all unmeasured on
     that route, and belong to the design's own measurements.

=== 01:39 UTC 2026-09-19 CORRECTION AND PRE-REGISTRATION -- REASONING EFFORT ON THE ROUTE THE PRODUCT ACTUALLY USES (gpt-5.6-luna)
CORRECTION first, because it changes what the three blocks above mean. They call arm A (a hand-built request to
/v1/chat/completions at effort none) "what the product sends today". I never measured that. The product does not
build its own HTTP request: its provider calls litellm, and litellm 1.86.2 moves a chat call for a gpt-5.4+ model onto
the Responses API whenever function tools ride with a reasoning effort that is not Python None
(litellm.main.responses_api_bridge_check; the string "none" counts). The provider's own 2026-09-17 rule sends exactly
that string with every tool-carrying call on this tier. Read offline from the installed library, no network: luna
with tools and effort "none" -> mode responses; "low" -> responses; effort unset -> chat; no tools -> chat.
Measured on the wire 2026-09-19 at 01:18Z, 6 requests, with a send hook that records the URL path and the body's
effort field and never a header:
  chat-none         litellm, plain openai/gpt-5.6-luna, one tool, effort none   POST /v1/responses 200, effort none
  chat-low          the same at effort low                                      POST /v1/responses 200, effort low
  product-complete  the product's provider, buffered, nothing configured        POST /v1/responses 200, effort none
  product-stream    the same, streamed, which is what a served app does         POST /v1/responses 200, effort none
  bridge-low-loop   litellm's explicit responses/ prefix, effort low, 2 turns   both 200; the second request carries
                    the function_call and function_call_output items, and the model answered it
  In every request the system text rode as `instructions` and the tool arrived flattened. No request went to
  /v1/chat/completions. The product's two requests carry the same body keys (input, instructions,
  max_output_tokens, model, reasoning, stream, tool_choice, tools). 0 key-shaped strings in the probe's output.
So today's product sits on arm C's ROUTE (the Responses API at effort none), and litellm renders the request much
as my arm C does: the system message becomes `instructions` and tools are flattened. Three rendering differences
are on record: litellm sends `strict: null` on each tool where arm C sends no strict key (read offline from its
converter), the product adds max_output_tokens and tool_choice auto, and arm C sends `store: false`. Whether any of
them moves behaviour is what Q1 below reads.
What stands in the blocks above: every count, every exact test, and the B-against-C readings. What does not stand:
the phrase "against today's product" wherever it meant arm A. Batch 1 and the replication read B against A as
"order no room, refusal +14 and +17"; against C, the route the product is on, the same two batches read order 19
against 6 and 18 against 8, refusal 20 against 0 twice. I do not re-label the verdict from those numbers: C is my
hand-built request, not the product's, and this block measures the product's own.

A consequence of the route, measured the same hour because it is live in production. None of those requests carries
a `store` field, and the product never sets one. On the Responses API that means stored: a request with no `store`
field came back `store: true` and was retrievable by its id afterwards (GET 200; I deleted it, DELETE 200), and the
same request with `store: false` came back `store: false` and its id was a 404. Chat completions stores only on
request (the documented default, not measured here), so for luna tool calls this has held at least since the
2026-09-17 rule began sending effort none with tools, which is what moves them onto the bridge. terra takes the same
branch in the offline check and was not sent. The opt-out has one working door: a top-level `store=False` handed
to litellm never reaches the wire (measured: no `store` key in the body), while `extra_body={"store": false}` does,
through litellm alone and through the product's provider, where the cache routing key already rides that door
(measured: both on the wire together). The fix is a product change with its own decision record, not part of this
bench. The P arms below are sent exactly as the product sends them, so their responses are stored; they carry
synthetic prompts only. The raw B and C arms have sent `store: false` since the first batch.

Why it matters: the served loop's stall signature is a text-only completion at a decision point (sample 4, 2026-09-19:
three turns ended veto_stall after text-only completions), and the one arm that showed that signature most in
isolation is the route the product turns out to be on.

